WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.732
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.732 --> 00:00:04.190
BRETT HESTERBERG:
Today we're going

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:05.930
to be joined by Peter Bakkum.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:08.335
Peter is the platform
lead at Quizlet.

00:00:08.335 --> 00:00:09.710
And the two of us
are going to be

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:14.090
talking about running
open-source databases on Google

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:15.530
Cloud Platform.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.480
So a few goals for today.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.040
We'll take a look
at what we view

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.664
as some of the most popular
open-source databases

00:00:22.664 --> 00:00:24.080
in the market right
now, and we'll

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.710
look at how you go about running
those databases on Google Cloud

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:28.250
Platform.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.350
We'll take a look at
what Google offers,

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.810
what some of our partners
offer with respect to managed

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.000
services, and we'll
look at how you can

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.286
self-manage these databases.

00:00:37.286 --> 00:00:38.660
And then Peter
will join onstage,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.050
and he'll talk about lessons
learned by Quizlet with respect

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.250
to running MySQL on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:50.060
Quizlet generally has a really
impressive MySQL architecture,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.770
and I know I'm
excited that he's here

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.370
to share some of their
learnings through, I think,

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.530
some pain and
certainly some success,

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.950
as well, running MySQL
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:04.610
So before I jump in, let me just
ask a question to all of you.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.640
By a show of hands,
how many developers

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.050
do we have in the audience?

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:10.550
OK.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.810
So about a little more than
half the room, maybe even 2/3.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.691
How many database administrators
do we have in the room?

00:01:17.691 --> 00:01:18.190
A few.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.312
How many reluctant
database administrators

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:21.270
do we have in the room?

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.320
There's a few more.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.942
[LAUGHS] And how many managers
do we have in the room?

00:01:25.942 --> 00:01:26.650
We got a handful.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:26.740
OK.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:27.240
Great.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.780
Well, I hope the
presentation will

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.517
have something for everyone,
and, indeed, I think it will.

00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:32.600
So let's get started here.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.630
We'll take a look at
popular databases,

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.550
and we're going to start with
the analyst view of the world.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:41.510
This is an analyst's view
of the database market,

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.560
as measured by revenue
or by market share.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.990
The bars you see on this
slide are not to scale.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.930
If you look leftmost, we
have our database market

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.630
at just over $35 billion.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.090
And we compare that to
the open-source database

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:01.840
market and the NoSQL markets
at 300 and change million.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.150
So if we actually
represented those

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.510
at scale, or in relative scale,
those would be, like, a pixel,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.419
and you wouldn't be able to
pick up the numbers there.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:13.030
The take on this, at
least from my perspective,

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.032
is that the analysts
are measuring a market

00:02:16.032 --> 00:02:16.990
in a very rational way.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:20.200
They're looking at dollars
spent, or money spent,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.940
on databases, usually in the
form of database licenses

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:28.600
and also in support contracts
and associated services.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.510
Historically, this has very much
benefited commercial databases

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:35.620
that often come with
large license fees.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.140
I think the reports
like this tend

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.830
to understate the significance
of open-source databases

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.254
in business, and this is
starting to change now.

00:02:44.254 --> 00:02:45.670
This view of the
world is starting

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.370
to change in that cloud
vendors like Google, some

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.830
of our partners,
other cloud vendors,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:55.540
are offering database services
based on open-source databases.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.750
And they're finding significant
interest from customers,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.260
and that's generating revenue.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.059
So these bars are starting
to change over time.

00:03:03.059 --> 00:03:04.600
I honestly think
what we have here is

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.410
kind of a massive shadow market,
where, if you surveyed everyone

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.660
running an open-source
database, you've

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.930
got a very large number of
users depending day to day

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.340
on open-source databases.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.120
And it's not reflected in
these type of reports yet.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.880
OK, with that in mind, we're
talking about the smaller

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.610
segments on this chart, our
open-source database charts.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:27.190
We, Google, have a very
casual relationship

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:28.990
with a company
called DB-Engines.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.570
DB-Engines looks at signals.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:35.500
Things like job postings,
development activity,

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.810
and a number of other
signals to determine

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.850
how popular, relatively, are
databases one to another.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.610
What we're showing here
is a list of databases.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.680
You can see rightmost
column, we've

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.850
highlighted relational databases
like MySQL, and Postgres,

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:53.830
for example, along with MariaDB.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:57.040
We've highlighted some of
the NoSQL databases, Mongo,

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.230
and some new SQL databases,
Cassandra, Redis,

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.030
Elasticsearch, HBase.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:04.240
And what you see is kind
of a relative popularity.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.840
Note that the y-axis
here is in log scale.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.570
So you have MySQL
at the top, kind

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.390
of the most popular
open-source database,

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.540
at least as measured
by database engines.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.880
And you see some
others that are growing

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:19.959
in popularity over time.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.540
Now given the log scale, you've
got something starting down

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.050
on the y-axis at 2, while
MySQL is above 1,000,

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.084
so bear that in mind.

00:04:27.084 --> 00:04:28.750
What we've seen in
the relational space,

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.520
very steady growth in terms
of popularity for a database

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.277
engine like PostgreSQL.

00:04:34.277 --> 00:04:35.860
And you can see a
number of the others

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.420
that I've highlighted
here growing

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.300
in popularity over time.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.010
What we'll look at
as we go forward

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:45.046
are these databases,
what options Google

00:04:45.046 --> 00:04:46.420
has for you with
respect to these

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.960
databases in Google Cloud,
what options our partners bring

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.180
in terms of managed
services, and how you can get

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:55.270
started self-managing
one of these databases.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.250
And what I'll note here is as
a product manager for Cloud

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.400
SQL, which is a managed
database service,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.480
I certainly have a bias.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.910
I love managed
database services.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:07.060
But I think what it comes down
to in terms of selecting--

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.180
do I self-manage a database,
do I go to a managed database

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:10.990
service--

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:16.300
is really the trade-off between
customization and that time

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.830
and toil in management.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.020
When I think about
looking at a database

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.026
service versus
self-managing, I think

00:05:22.026 --> 00:05:24.490
you all are aware of the
trade-offs you're making day

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.900
to day with your time.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.960
As developers, as DBAs, as
managers, you are deciding,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.250
I am going to customize this
part of my technology stack.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.170
And I'm willing
to spend the time

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.570
to maintain that
part of the stack.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.610
Other parts of the
stack you may say,

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:43.820
I'd like to turn this over to
someone else, and, in doing so,

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.820
I'm going to give up some
of the customization I

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.202
can get by managing it myself.

00:05:47.202 --> 00:05:48.910
I think that's the
trade-off you look at.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:50.639
So as we go through
these options,

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:52.180
hopefully they simply
illuminate some

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.471
of the options you have with
respect to these databases

00:05:54.471 --> 00:05:55.990
on Google Cloud.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:57.714
And then Peter
will join onstage,

00:05:57.714 --> 00:06:00.130
and Peter will take you through
their rationale, Quizlet's

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.650
rationale, for
self-managing MySQL and some

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.740
of the experiences they've
had on Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:09.460
So let's start with
MySQL and MariaDB,

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.230
which some will
call it MySQL clone,

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.480
certainly a fork of MySQL.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.910
For the Google-offered
service here,

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.840
Cloud SQL, our managed
relational database service,

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.750
has for years offered MySQL.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:23.550
Cloud SQL, we say,
is compatibility.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.320
But more than
compatibility-- we are

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.450
more than just a compatibility
layer, I should say.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:31.290
We are offering standard
MySQL databases,

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:35.310
and Cloud SQL takes care of
often mundane maintenance

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:35.910
operations.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.500
Things like patching
the database,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.960
patching the underlying
operating system,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.870
making replication feel
easy or click a button.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.250
We monitor high
availability, for example,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.855
and you're backed by
Google's team with respect

00:06:47.855 --> 00:06:48.730
to high availability.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.670
So it goes beyond
just the tooling

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.920
to make sure that a database
is up, and if it's not,

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.120
that we're failing over.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.890
You're getting
someone, in the end,

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:59.900
looking at these systems 24 by
7 so that you don't have to.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.500
That's kind of the essence of
the managed database service.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:06.540
What I will note about Cloud
SQL is that it is not a DBA.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.230
The hope with Cloud SQL is that,
for developers, it offers you

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:12.630
a chance to develop, to focus
more on your development,

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:14.490
rather than on
database management.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.130
For DBAs, I hope
it allows DBAs who

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.860
choose to use Cloud SQL
a chance to do something

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.600
a bit more higher
value with their time

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.700
than installing or
provisioning a database,

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.870
or patching a
database, for example.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.010
We have a partner in ClearDB
offering managed services

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.240
in this very space, as well.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:34.260
And we have, we note, in
our launcher platform,

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.350
which is pre-built
images or OS images that

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:42.840
include an installation of
MySQL or Percona or other MySQL

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.070
relational databases.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:45.390
We have those options for you.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.480
So as you move down
this chart, you'll

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.470
see this again and again
as I go through these,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.960
we have Google's managed
service, a partner's managed

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.000
service, and options
for you to quickly

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.487
get started self-hosting
these databases.

00:07:57.487 --> 00:07:58.570
Taking a look at Postgres.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.800
Cloud SQL, as of yesterday
at this conference,

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.100
announced support for the
first time for PostgreSQL.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.930
It was a longstanding
request, and we're

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.660
very happy to be saying
yes to that request now.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.660
So Cloud SQL for Postrgres
just went beta as of yesterday,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.340
is launching right now.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:17.160
We have ElephantDB, who
is running a managed

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.570
service as a partner.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:21.300
And you see in the launcher
space here offerings

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.020
from EnterpriseDB,
strong Postgres partner;

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.960
from Aiven, who is offering
a managed service as well;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.620
and we have Bitnami
images, which,

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.780
effectively, package a Postrgres
installation into an image.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.750
So you can quickly get
started with Postgres.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.539
One thing to note on the
DB-Engines ranking here,

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:41.730
you see the rise in
Postgres over time.

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:43.289
I think the momentum
behind Postgres

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:44.870
right now is really exciting.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.310
It has a long history
of active development,

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.800
and we're excited,
as Google, now

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.680
to have an offering in
the managed space here.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.720
Taking a look at
Cassandra and HBase.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.060
Google has a product
called Bigtable, which

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.350
I hope you've all heard of.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.340
It has an HBase API layer.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.340
So when we talk about
HBase at Google,

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:06.600
we refer to Bigtable as
our product in this space.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.190
In terms of managed
service offerings,

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:13.200
our partner, Instaclustr, has
offerings around the Cassandra

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:13.860
space.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.240
And then, of course, we have
launcher images, both Cassandra

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.100
proper, as well as
from DataStax, to help

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.960
get you started with Cassandra.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.920
And you can see some interesting
movement in the Cassandra

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.650
and HBase rankings in the
DB-Engines chart to the right.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:31.800
Looking at MongoDB,
Cloud Datastore's

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.750
our offering in this space.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:35.600
Datastore is often
paired with App Engine,

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.350
for those of you who
are familiar with it.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.840
But Datastore is
great when it comes

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.720
to big scale and,
especially, document storage.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.760
Looking at managed
service offerings,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:49.200
Mongo Labs has an offering, as
well as Compose and MongoDB.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.920
In the launcher space, again,
we have standard Mongo options

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.050
and MongoDB with
replication to help

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:58.623
you get going rapidly with
MongoDB on the Google Cloud

00:09:58.623 --> 00:10:00.050
Platform.

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:01.840
And looking now at Redis.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.750
We've recently unveiled
App Engine Memcache.

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:07.960
I think you will continue to
see us innovate in this space.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:10.480
We think in-memory databases
are important and caching

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:11.950
layer's important there.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:14.180
The managed service
offerings we have

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:17.850
are via partnerships with Redis
Labs and again with Aiven.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.960
And we've got some
representation from Aiven,

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:22.570
I think, in the audience
potentially today, as well.

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:24.070
Looking at our
launcher space, we've

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:26.680
got classic Redis as one of
our launcher applications.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:29.800
If you haven't been to
Cloud Launcher, take a look.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:32.500
We see customers getting
some value out of Launcher

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.410
even just for simple
things like testing.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.974
And then those images can
certainly end up in production.

00:10:37.974 --> 00:10:39.640
But it's just a quick
way to get started

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:43.360
with some of these
applications on Google Cloud.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.030
So I'm very happy to
now be introducing

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:47.560
Peter Bakkum from Quizlet.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.750
Peter's the platform
lead, and Quizlet

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.720
is a heavy user of
MySQL, and generally,

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:55.120
a heavy user of the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.220
So Peter, I think, has
a great story to tell

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:58.750
and hopefully some
lessons learned

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.720
that you can all benefit
from with respect to running

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:04.720
MySQL on Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.722
So please join me
in welcoming Peter.

00:11:07.722 --> 00:11:10.202
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:10.202 --> 00:11:13.180
PETER BAKKUM: Thanks.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:14.560
Everybody, I'm Peter.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.400
I work on platform at Quizlet.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:19.300
And I'm really excited
to be here today.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:23.320
I think to preface this,
there's an implication

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:24.886
if I'm standing
up here that we've

00:11:24.886 --> 00:11:26.260
solved all these
problems, and we

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:28.010
have this perfect,
great infrastructure

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:28.920
that never goes down.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.480
And I definitely
don't feel that way.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:33.430
But I think the
value in these talks

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.440
is just for us to give
you information about how

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.430
we run our infrastructure,
and hopefully, my goal

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:42.640
is you'll come away from this
with just a few more ideas

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:43.932
and some context of what we do.

00:11:43.932 --> 00:11:45.306
And hopefully that
would help you

00:11:45.306 --> 00:11:46.812
if you're running
something similar.

00:11:46.812 --> 00:11:47.770
So that's my goal here.

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:49.603
I'm going to give you
a bunch of information

00:11:49.603 --> 00:11:52.660
about our architecture, some
decisions we've made, how we

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:54.380
can figure things, et cetera.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:56.156
And hopefully it'll be useful.

00:11:56.156 --> 00:11:58.030
And there's going to be
questions at the end.

00:11:58.030 --> 00:11:59.446
If you don't get
your question in,

00:11:59.446 --> 00:12:02.320
I'll be around, as well,
just to chat about it,

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:04.040
if you're interested
in doing that.

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:08.800
So I'd like to start with just
a brief introduction to Quizlet,

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.450
if you haven't heard of us.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:13.080
Quizlet is an ed tech company.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.380
We make a study tool that
you plug your content into,

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:19.180
and then it helps you practice
and master that content.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:22.756
So we've had really strong
adoption in high schools,

00:12:22.756 --> 00:12:23.880
colleges, things like that.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:28.020
It's really good for vocabulary,
foreign language, et cetera.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:30.750
We have web and
mobile native apps.

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.670
And some stats on us.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:35.630
We have about 25
million users a month,

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:39.960
and on Quantcast, we're
like a top 25 US website.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.660
So we've grown from
something pretty small

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:47.850
into something that's having
a major impact on education.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.550
So this has been our
growth curve over time.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:54.925
So we're really lucky to
have strong organic growth.

00:12:54.925 --> 00:12:56.550
But the things I want
to point out here

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:59.430
is this is a challenging
domain to run databases in.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.500
So you can see we're
an education company.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:02.820
It's very, very seasonal.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:05.490
So no one studies over
the summer, it turns out.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:07.130
It's pretty quiet.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:08.880
So we get this big
drop off in the summer,

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.280
and then every year
in August, September

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:14.490
we hit back to school
when US students return

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:17.950
to high schools, universities,
and we get this big jump.

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:20.040
So that's also where
our growth hits.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:25.470
So summer is-- we really
focus on load testing,

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:28.590
preparing for that rush,
and effectively, we

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.090
are preparing for
a level of traffic

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:31.590
that we've never really seen.

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:34.410
So this is difficult. We get
these big seasonal swings

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:39.000
of traffic, and we need to
manage and deal with this.

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:45.600
It's really important that
we stay up and stable.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.790
If we go down, this
is what happens.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:49.620
This is a tweet of a classroom.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.110
All the students are
holding iPads that

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.074
say Quizlet is down on them.

00:13:54.074 --> 00:13:56.490
And actually, the projector
in the back says that as well.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:59.490
And so I put this up
here just to empathize

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.100
with the user a little
bit and underline

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.690
why we feel this
is important to us

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:05.670
and why we care
about running this

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:09.240
really stably and performantly.

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.350
And avoiding this
kind of situation,

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:14.520
which is hard to see
when this happens, right?

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:15.570
So that's why we're here.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:22.160
Like many applications,
Quizlet started pretty simple.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.730
It was our founder,
Andrew, wrote it to help

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.210
himself study in high school.

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:28.250
And when we started,
it was pretty simple.

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:31.520
It was just an application in
a database, written in PHP,

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:33.410
communicating with MySQL.

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:35.540
And as we've grown, as
we've hit that growth

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:39.470
curve over the past 10 years,
we've had to scale this up.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:43.440
We've had to re-architect and
solve this scaling challenge.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:45.810
So what have we done?

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:49.550
So this may be a pretty
familiar list to a lot of you.

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:52.710
We've done the basic
stuff-- we've added indexes,

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.290
we've optimized our queries.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.300
We've done things like
eliminating joins,

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:02.160
so our queries almost
always hit a single table.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.800
We've moved to bigger
hardware, so we've just

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.270
scaled up vertically.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.730
And we started
caching query results.

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.250
I'm going to talk a little
bit how we use Memcached.

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:13.090
We've moved to partition tables.

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:17.670
So we've taken a
single table, and we've

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.620
used MySQL partitioning
to split that

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:23.760
into multiple individual
files on disk.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.060
We've compressed table data.

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:28.320
This helps with Disk I/O.

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:33.390
And probably our
biggest scaling effort

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:36.330
has been around taking
tables and splitting them out

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:37.374
into their own hardware.

00:15:37.374 --> 00:15:38.790
So this is just
vertical sharding.

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:43.810
So our biggest tables we
run on separate instances.

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:47.410
Once you do this, you have
a more complex architecture,

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:51.210
but you get much,
much more scale.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:52.740
Because we use
replication, we've

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:54.772
started running queries
on replica sets.

00:15:54.772 --> 00:15:56.730
So these are the queries
that don't necessarily

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:58.530
need to be exactly consistent.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:01.890
So if you do a write
and you do a read,

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:05.250
they don't necessarily need
to be completely up to date.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.780
But this is a really
nice scaling strategy.

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:09.390
And once you do
this, you can then

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:12.150
increase the number of
replicas and scale this way.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:13.920
So this has been our strategy.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:15.840
As I said, I'm sure
that some of you

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:18.747
are probably familiar with this
and have done similar things.

00:16:18.747 --> 00:16:21.330
And I want to talk a little bit
about the implications of this

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.490
on our architecture
and what this actually

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:26.260
looks like to run on GCP.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.300
So here's our
current architecture.

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:30.430
This is just a very
high-level diagram.

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:31.270
We have the web app.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:35.230
It's talking to these pods
of master replica pairs.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:38.137
We run the replicas so that
we can swap between when

00:16:38.137 --> 00:16:39.220
we need to do maintenance.

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:41.230
Or if there's a production
issue on the master,

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:44.380
we're able to failover and
promote the replica to a master

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:45.517
and run that way.

00:16:45.517 --> 00:16:47.600
The important part is the
replica is always there,

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:50.950
and it's always ready
if we need to swap over.

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:53.000
We would run a separate
replica for backups.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:54.958
And we snap shot this
replica, and I'll explain

00:16:54.958 --> 00:16:56.610
a little bit more about that.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.360
As I mentioned before,
we've taken tables,

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:00.234
and we've split them
into their own hardware.

00:17:00.234 --> 00:17:03.340
So we now run a production,
eight of these pods

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:05.040
of master replica pairs.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.380
And in some of these pods,
like in the middle here,

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:09.130
we run multiple replicas.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.390
So we've scaled in that way.

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:14.079
And then finally, we run
this tier of caching.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:17.440
And the application
communicates with this pool

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:20.290
of Memcached machines,
and this alleviates

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:22.240
some of the load on MySQL.

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:25.640
I'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:28.280
So what does our
workload look like?

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:31.360
As I mentioned, we've split
out into separate pods.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:34.000
We have about 3 terabytes
of production data,

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.900
and if you add in the indexes,
that's another 2.2 terabytes.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:40.151
So I think of this as kind
of a medium-sized workload.

00:17:40.151 --> 00:17:41.525
There's many MySQL
workloads that

00:17:41.525 --> 00:17:43.810
are much, much larger than
this, but this is basically

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:46.980
the domain we've scaled into.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:48.920
During an average
week, we peak around

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:50.940
28,000 queries per
second, and that's

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:52.860
spread across all
of our databases,

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:54.490
across all of the replicas.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:57.210
And in total, we have
about 40 billion rows,

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:59.070
and that's across 100 tables.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:02.790
So whenever we add a feature
or something like that,

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:05.130
we often end up with
an additional table,

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:10.480
and so the app is communicating
with about 100 tables in total.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:13.090
So this is the shape
of the workload.

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:16.390
And there's a bunch
of entities in there.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:19.790
That 40 billion rows,
it's both the content--

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:24.250
so at Quizlet our content is
term and definition pairs.

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:25.630
That's what people are studying.

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:29.110
But it's also some
more analytical things.

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:32.920
So we record every answer that
anyone punches into Quizlet,

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:35.930
and we can use this to
help people study later on.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.302
So this is important
data, and it's important

00:18:38.302 --> 00:18:40.510
that it's in the production
database rather than just

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:43.576
an analytical database so we
can use it in the application

00:18:43.576 --> 00:18:44.950
and help drive
studying later on.

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:50.520
So this is our traffic
during an average week.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:52.186
I think this is pretty
common with a lot

00:18:52.186 --> 00:18:56.580
of consumer-facing companies,
but this is starting Saturday.

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.040
So you can see, not much
studying on Saturday.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:00.720
Sunday night we start
seeing this hump.

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:04.291
And then during the week,
we see a bunch of traffic

00:19:04.291 --> 00:19:06.540
in the morning-- this is
when people are using Quizlet

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:08.550
in school and the
classroom-- and then

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:10.100
we see another hump at night.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:11.970
And so this is when
people are studying

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:13.290
at night for the next day.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:16.020
So you'll notice that we don't
see that evening hump on Friday

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:16.520
night.

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:23.370
So I think one question
that I would have

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:25.560
is, why are you guys doing this?

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:27.370
Why aren't you
running Cloud SQL?

00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:28.470
It's the managed service.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:31.060
It would solve a lot
of these problems.

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:34.620
So my philosophy on this is
you should use managed services

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:36.300
whenever and wherever
you can, and they

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:39.390
have a huge, huge
potential to save you time,

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:42.480
save you money, save you
operational costs, et cetera.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:46.860
We moved over to GCP a couple
of years ago, and at the time

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:50.250
we needed larger machines
than Cloud SQL offered.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:53.850
And we've continued to
scale up to larger machines,

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:58.440
and so we're now
running on 64 core VMs.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:00.030
We'd also had
operational experience

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:01.980
running MySQL on
our previous cloud,

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:04.110
and so we had a
relationship with Percona,

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.071
and we've tuned our
databases specifically

00:20:06.071 --> 00:20:07.320
for our workload with Percona.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:10.690
So some of the configurations
was specific to us,

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:14.760
and we wanted to continue
having control over those.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.490
We also had experience managing
the replication and failover.

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:20.640
And we like to control
that, because we know where

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:24.630
you are in the recovery step and
what the replication is doing

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:27.510
and being able to handle
that kind of promotion

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:28.740
and that kind of thing.

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:31.290
There's a maintenance cost
there and complexity costs,

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:34.860
but we wanted to be able
to handle that ourselves.

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:36.810
But as I said, we're
very, very optimistic

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:39.570
about hosted infrastructure,
and we use it wherever we can.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.810
And we'll likely revisit how
we run this stuff over time.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:50.140
So how have we configured this?

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:52.610
What does this looks like?

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:55.700
So we run Percona MySQL 5.7.

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:57.890
This is a recent
Percona release.

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:02.120
And as I said, we run a bunch
of different machine sizes.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:05.190
So the smaller machines are the
backup machines, our 8 core,

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:06.860
they're the standard-8's.

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:09.590
And this scales up
to 64-core machines.

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:11.930
So we're using the
highmem-64 cores, which

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:13.990
are about 400 gigs of memory.

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:15.740
And those are where
we need the most power

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:18.110
and we have the biggest
database workloads.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:21.050
We're using the Google,
just vanilla PD-SSD drives,

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:22.700
so this is 25,000 IOPS.

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:25.010
And so we've
optimized our workload

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:26.840
around that ceiling
and that number,

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:30.120
and it's important to us.

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.610
We're also using
GTID-based replication.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.910
So the thing to know
about that, if you're not

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:36.950
familiar with that,
GTID replication

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:39.170
is a way of tracking
the transactions that

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:42.800
have been sent to a replica
and then executed on a replica.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:45.410
And they're important because
if you have two databases that

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:47.690
have the same GTID
executions, then

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:50.460
you can be pretty sure that they
have the same database state.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:53.630
So it's a really convenient
way of managing this,

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:56.720
and when we're
swapping in replicas,

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:01.000
we ensure that there's
GTID consistency there.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:02.580
So I just wanted
to call that out

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.260
as an important piece of this.

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:07.670
We've done a number of
things to tune the workload.

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:10.780
And the things that I want to
highlight there are the biggest

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:14.620
configuration changes we've made
from a performance standpoint

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:19.090
have been to tune the buffer
pool sizes and optimize this,

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:21.250
and tune the number
of buffer pools.

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:24.280
So buffer pools,
as you may know,

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:27.250
it's how MySQL is
caching pages in memory.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:31.480
So it's a MySQL-managed
cache, and we

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:36.324
tune this to be around 80% of
the memory on these machines.

00:22:36.324 --> 00:22:37.740
When you're running
[? machines ?]

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.000
with a number of cores--

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:41.172
so 64 cores you're
going to end up

00:22:41.172 --> 00:22:43.380
with a number of worker
threads, and the buffer pools

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:44.370
are a shared resource.

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.800
And so if you don't scale
the number of buffer pools

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:49.290
with the number of CPU cores,
you end up with contention.

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:51.331
So we found it very
important for our performance

00:22:51.331 --> 00:22:53.250
to optimize a number of
buffer pool instances.

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:58.380
And the current number
we use is two CPU cores

00:22:58.380 --> 00:22:59.860
to a buffer pool instance.

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:01.620
Though we may experiment
more with that.

00:23:08.789 --> 00:23:11.330
So next I want to talk about
how we access this from the app.

00:23:11.330 --> 00:23:13.663
So what does it look like on
the ground when the Quizlet

00:23:13.663 --> 00:23:17.000
app actually talks to MySQL?

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:20.310
So Quizlet was originally
written in PHP.

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:23.960
It was originally just
a PHP MySQL application.

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:26.330
I think a lot of
apps start this way.

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:28.740
PHP is not the favorite
language of many people.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:32.570
And so what we've done
is try to optimize how we

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:34.160
use it, improve how we use it.

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:36.110
And the state we're
in now is we're

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:41.570
using HHVM, which is Facebook's
PHP just-in-time compiler.

00:23:41.570 --> 00:23:43.702
It carries a number
of advantages.

00:23:43.702 --> 00:23:46.160
We were able to significantly
reduce our machine footprint.

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:48.170
We cut our number of web
servers by about half

00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:50.690
by switching to HHVM.

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:54.350
And it also introduces a
layer of type information.

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:56.750
So if you're just
running vanilla PHP,

00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:59.210
HHVM attempts to detect
the types you're using

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:01.280
and optimize based on that.

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:03.470
We've also begun using a
language called Hack, which

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:06.870
is based on HHVM, and it
carries a number of features,

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:10.970
but at its simplest,
it's like a typed PHP.

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:13.280
So we've transitioned a
lot of our code into Hack,

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:15.050
and this has had
a lot of benefits,

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:18.410
and the application developers
are pretty happy about this.

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:22.610
So application running in HHVM,
we're communicating with MySQL

00:24:22.610 --> 00:24:28.910
via HHVM PDO, which is an
extension written in C++ that

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:32.210
handles details of connections,
queries, et cetera,

00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:33.870
and communicates
directly with MySQL.

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:37.370
So all of our queries are
going through HHVM PDO.

00:24:37.370 --> 00:24:41.810
Finally, moving a layer up,
we're running custom ORM.

00:24:41.810 --> 00:24:48.860
So within our application,
this big HHVM application,

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:50.720
queries are going
through this ORM.

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:53.720
As you can see, I've included
a sample query at the bottom

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:55.160
here.

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:58.370
This is a fluent interface we
have for composing queries.

00:24:58.370 --> 00:25:03.560
You can do things like add query
predicates, filters, et cetera.

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:05.450
You can mark a query
as read scalable, which

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:07.820
means it's OK to
send to the replica,

00:25:07.820 --> 00:25:09.239
and it doesn't need consistency.

00:25:09.239 --> 00:25:11.030
And then this is going
to handle the actual

00:25:11.030 --> 00:25:14.850
composing the SQL query and
sending the results back.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:17.437
So this is how we're
actually forming the query,

00:25:17.437 --> 00:25:19.020
communicating back
to the application.

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:21.080
This is what our developers
are interacting with.

00:25:25.330 --> 00:25:27.222
So I want to talk about
caching for a second.

00:25:27.222 --> 00:25:29.430
This, I think, is an
interesting piece of this puzzle

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:32.720
because caching gets
a little tricky.

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:34.560
I want to explain why.

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:36.240
We run a pool of
Memcached machines.

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:39.300
I think this is a pretty
standard architecture.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:41.610
We do consistent
hashing, so each node,

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:44.880
each web node is communicating
with each of the Memcached

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:46.890
nodes.

00:25:46.890 --> 00:25:49.860
And we're hashing
consistently, so each key

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:53.120
from all the web nodes ends
up on the same Memcached node.

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:59.620
So we're basically utilizing
Memcached as a cache for MySQL.

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:01.750
The advantage here is
we reduce query load,

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:03.570
so we're sending fewer
queries to MySQL,

00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:05.770
but we're also, because
we're storing this in cache,

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:09.400
we are freeing up a lot of the
MySQL cache for other queries.

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:12.280
So things that would have
been going in the buffer pool,

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:14.990
increasing buffer pool
turnover, et cetera,

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:16.900
are instead just
going to Memcached.

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:19.840
So this is great,
but this gets tricky.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:25.660
Remember, there's
really only one source

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:26.630
of truth for our data.

00:26:26.630 --> 00:26:29.110
So MySQL is a source
of truth for our data.

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:30.420
The whole thing rests on MySQL.

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.640
It's our transactional,
relational database.

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:37.630
And fundamentally, if
we do a transaction,

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:39.880
if it fails to
write to Memcached,

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:44.310
the transaction still succeeds,
but Memcached is out of sync.

00:26:44.310 --> 00:26:46.550
So this is
particularly important

00:26:46.550 --> 00:26:48.860
because we're using
Memcached as a model cache.

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:55.364
So literally, our models
in the application,

00:26:55.364 --> 00:26:56.780
we're serializing
those, and we're

00:26:56.780 --> 00:26:59.600
storing those in Memcached.

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:01.940
And so when those get
out of sync, you can see,

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:04.310
you immediately
have inconsistency

00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:05.690
in the application.

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:09.500
So we mitigate this partially
by setting a TTL on everything

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:10.790
we write to Memcached.

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:15.450
So effectively,
when this happens,

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:17.690
there's a limit on the
inconsistency you could see.

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:20.510
But it's important to
keep this in mind--

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:22.295
there's the possibility
of inconsistency.

00:27:22.295 --> 00:27:24.170
For example, if there's
a network disruption,

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:25.754
you'd see this.

00:27:25.754 --> 00:27:27.920
And certain things you don't
want in this framework.

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:30.680
So, for example, queries
around billing, right?

00:27:30.680 --> 00:27:31.930
You don't want that to happen.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:33.548
That's really, really critical.

00:27:33.548 --> 00:27:38.390
So we utilize caching,
I think, pragmatically.

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:41.720
We think it's really beneficial
to reduce the load on MySQL.

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:44.690
But it's really important
to keep this in mind

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:45.830
when we're using it.

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:48.350
And we've played
around a lot with this

00:27:48.350 --> 00:27:51.410
about where do we
set the TTL, where do

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:53.162
we bust the cache, et cetera.

00:27:53.162 --> 00:27:54.620
There's another
layer of complexity

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:58.100
here, that if you re-balance
the cache buckets,

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:00.020
and then you re-balance
again, you also

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:01.550
have the chance of stale data.

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:03.890
So we've spent a
lot of time thinking

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:07.940
about this, about how we
manage the cache pool.

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:09.860
And just attempting
to be thoughtful

00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:11.750
and avoid this situation.

00:28:11.750 --> 00:28:16.950
What I'm getting at is,
basically, if a Memcached

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.840
node drops, what do you do?

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:23.150
One perspective, maybe,
you re-balance your cache,

00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:24.920
but if this is
flapping, you can end up

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:26.840
with a lot of stale data.

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:29.220
So we actually never re-balance
the cache when we use it.

00:28:32.167 --> 00:28:34.250
So next I want to talk
about our database failover

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:35.930
infrastructure.

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:38.750
What I mean by this
is we frequently

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:41.690
need to failover from
a master to a replica

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:44.930
and promote the replica
to the new master.

00:28:44.930 --> 00:28:47.660
This is tricky because
fundamentally you

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:50.012
need to ensure data
consistency, and you

00:28:50.012 --> 00:28:52.220
want to do this while you're
running the application.

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:54.040
You want to reduce downtime.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:56.540
You want to reduce the
problems you're seeing.

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:58.910
So here's how we handle this.

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:01.100
We're doing service
discovery with Consul.

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:04.400
And the way this
works is Consul holds

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:07.600
the master and the
replica, holds the IPs,

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:11.270
and then we're templating
files from Consul.

00:29:11.270 --> 00:29:15.350
So this means we're able to
change the value in consul,

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:20.860
and then this templates the
value into config files on all

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:22.340
of our application servers.

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:24.860
And this happens
really, really quickly.

00:29:24.860 --> 00:29:27.910
So that's how we do
the service discovery,

00:29:27.910 --> 00:29:29.710
but the application
still needs to be

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:33.400
aware of which is
the master when,

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:35.380
and we need to ensure
that there is consistency

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:36.700
when we do this failover.

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:37.866
This is a little bit tricky.

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:41.450
So here's our process for this.

00:29:44.550 --> 00:29:49.690
We kickoff this process,
we pick a replica.

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:52.630
We know we want to swap
over to this replica,

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:54.060
promote it to the master.

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:57.076
And the first step is
to snapshot the replica.

00:29:57.076 --> 00:29:58.450
So this will come
in useful later

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:00.670
on when we restart replication.

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:03.400
So we snapshot the
replica, we then

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:05.350
lock writes on
this whole system.

00:30:05.350 --> 00:30:08.290
So on the database
we're switching,

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:09.970
we stopped doing
writes, and this

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:12.430
is because we need to wait
for the replica to catch up.

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.280
We need to wait
for the failover.

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:16.180
So we lock writes.

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:21.370
This is critical because this
has a major impact on the app.

00:30:21.370 --> 00:30:23.730
The database that you're
doing this failover on,

00:30:23.730 --> 00:30:25.850
you're then effectively
not serving that to users.

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:30.810
So this has a major impact on
how the application is running.

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:33.590
So we lock writes, and then
we wait for the replication

00:30:33.590 --> 00:30:36.680
to catch up on the new master.

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:38.330
We're waiting.

00:30:38.330 --> 00:30:40.370
In practice, our
replicas are often

00:30:40.370 --> 00:30:45.890
within a second of the master
in terms of transactions.

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:49.370
But we want to be really extra
sure that they're caught up,

00:30:49.370 --> 00:30:51.650
because if you don't wait
for replication to catch up,

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:53.524
you're effectively
leaving writes behind when

00:30:53.524 --> 00:30:55.460
you swap to the new master.

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:58.432
So we wait for
replication to come up.

00:30:58.432 --> 00:31:00.140
We promote the replica
to the new master.

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:01.830
So this is where
Consul comes in.

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:03.850
This is where we change
the service discovery,

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:07.160
we're templating the files on
all the application servers,

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:10.980
and then they know that,
OK, this is now the master.

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:12.810
We then unlock this database.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:14.870
So this is the point
of no return, right?

00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:18.110
This means we are both
writing to the new master

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:19.770
and reading from it.

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:21.470
And so this is the
point of no return,

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:24.590
because you then can't go
back to the previous master

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:27.290
without losing writes.

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:30.400
So this is a critical
piece of this.

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:33.210
So we've unlocked, the
application comes back,

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:34.740
we're reading and writing.

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:37.050
And then the next
step we need to do

00:31:37.050 --> 00:31:39.576
is to restart replication
the other way.

00:31:39.576 --> 00:31:40.950
So in other words,
the old master

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:44.306
now needs to replicate
from the new master.

00:31:44.306 --> 00:31:45.930
This isn't strictly
necessary, but what

00:31:45.930 --> 00:31:49.110
we do to avoid replication
problems is that we then

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:53.070
restore the snapshot
from the old replica,

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:57.300
put that on the new replica,
so we ensure there's complete

00:31:57.300 --> 00:31:59.850
consistency, the
GTIDs match exactly,

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:01.650
and then we restart replication.

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:04.200
So now we're running fine,
we're running smoothly,

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:07.200
we're back in action.

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:08.880
This actually gets
even more complex

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:11.730
with multi-replica pools.

00:32:11.730 --> 00:32:13.230
So as you can
imagine, you then need

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:16.260
to pick one of the
replicas to failover to.

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:18.775
And any other replicas then
need to be aware of this process

00:32:18.775 --> 00:32:21.365
and point to the new one.

00:32:21.365 --> 00:32:22.740
So as I mentioned
before, there's

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.020
a lot of complexity
in managing this.

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:27.270
We have experience
with this, and we

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:28.560
have scripting around this.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:32.310
But with complexity, the
scripting is difficult to test,

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:34.110
because to test it
really thoroughly,

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:39.070
you need a very robust
staging or integration testing

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:40.036
environment.

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:47.010
The point I'd like to make is
just that there's complexity.

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:49.170
We manage it, because we
like having control of it,

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:51.720
we like knowing exactly
the characteristics of when

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:55.210
the failover is happening,
and being able to fix it

00:32:55.210 --> 00:32:56.130
if something breaks.

00:32:56.130 --> 00:32:58.827
But there's a cost to it.

00:32:58.827 --> 00:33:00.410
So next I want to
talk about something

00:33:00.410 --> 00:33:02.660
that's super important to us.

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:04.620
This is backups.

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:10.300
So we're replicating
data to another replica,

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:11.280
which is smaller.

00:33:11.280 --> 00:33:13.470
And the purpose of
this machine is just

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:17.040
to keep up with the
write load and keep us

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:19.140
a state with the master,
so in other words,

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:20.784
follow closely
behind the master.

00:33:20.784 --> 00:33:22.450
We're not reading
from this note at all.

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:27.256
This is one of my
favorite parts of GCP.

00:33:27.256 --> 00:33:28.880
This is one of the
things we liked most

00:33:28.880 --> 00:33:33.530
when we were evaluating GCP
as a potential platform.

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:35.480
The GCP snapshots
are very, very quick.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:39.380
So even multi-terabyte
SSD drives,

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:41.914
we can snapshot those literally
in a matter of minutes.

00:33:41.914 --> 00:33:43.580
This is pretty
significant, because what

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:47.720
it means is you can get a full
copy of a disk, consistent,

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:49.224
it's really, really
quick, and it's

00:33:49.224 --> 00:33:51.390
fast enough that we're able
to do this once an hour.

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:56.150
So if we were to lose the
running instances we have,

00:33:56.150 --> 00:33:58.580
the most data that we
would lose in that scenario

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:00.050
is an hour's worth of data.

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:01.060
So this is pretty nice.

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:03.740
We just press a snapshot
button, it goes, it saves,

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:06.840
and we have this guarantee.

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.721
So there's some
other things we want

00:34:08.721 --> 00:34:09.929
to do in this infrastructure.

00:34:09.929 --> 00:34:12.360
So one is we want to
test these backups.

00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:14.610
We want to make sure
that they're consistent,

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:17.880
they're sane, if we need them
we can bring them back in.

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:21.219
And we also want to store
some copies of this elsewhere.

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:23.909
So we want to store
copies in the event

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:28.949
that we would lose access to
GCE, what if we lost access

00:34:28.949 --> 00:34:31.889
to our entire GCP
account, et cetera?

00:34:31.889 --> 00:34:34.560
We want to solve
that problem as well.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:37.050
So this is what our
architecture looks like.

00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:39.719
As I mentioned, we have
this backup replica.

00:34:39.719 --> 00:34:42.360
This has an SSD that
we're snapshotting.

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:44.670
And then we do a couple
of things with this drive.

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:47.370
We take the snapshot,
we turn it into a disk,

00:34:47.370 --> 00:34:50.659
and then we mount it
on two other machines.

00:34:50.659 --> 00:34:52.850
So first is a backup tester.

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:56.900
All this machine does is it
looks for an untested backup.

00:34:56.900 --> 00:34:59.850
It brings it in, it
mounts the drive,

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:01.772
and it does the MySQL recovery.

00:35:01.772 --> 00:35:03.230
So once the MySQL
recovery is done,

00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:04.396
you can run queries on this.

00:35:04.396 --> 00:35:06.710
And we just run a
pretty simple query.

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:10.220
So we're making sure
the data is sane,

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:12.020
the tables are as
large as we expect,

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:15.770
we're seeing recent time stamps
on the tables, et cetera.

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:18.380
The worst case scenario here
is you try to do a backup,

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:20.240
and you realize your
backups are broken.

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:23.190
We do testing to ensure
you don't see that, right?

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:24.810
We also have an archiver.

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:27.580
And what this is doing is
it's mounting the drive,

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:31.484
it's tarballing up just
the raw binary MySQL files,

00:35:31.484 --> 00:35:33.650
and then it's shipping those
off into cloud storage.

00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:35.700
So we're shipping it
to GCS, and then we're

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:37.740
also shipping it
to S3 to ensure we

00:35:37.740 --> 00:35:40.050
have a backup that's just
outside of our GCP account

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:41.780
domain.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:44.960
So this is important
to us, because we

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:46.430
want to make sure
that if there's

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:50.330
a disaster we can recover, our
backups are sane, coherent,

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:52.725
we have copies
elsewhere, et cetera.

00:35:52.725 --> 00:35:54.600
So we've put some time
and thought into this.

00:35:57.970 --> 00:36:00.100
I also want to talk
about data warehousing.

00:36:00.100 --> 00:36:02.540
So this is great.

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:05.440
We've solved backups,
we've solved replication,

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:06.850
and failover, et cetera.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:12.350
But our data analysts,
they want this data.

00:36:12.350 --> 00:36:17.290
There's a class of queries
that MySQL is really not

00:36:17.290 --> 00:36:18.310
intended for, right?

00:36:18.310 --> 00:36:20.670
Analytical queries, queries
over your entire data

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:22.990
set, large scans, et cetera.

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:25.930
So at the scale we're
talking about of a database

00:36:25.930 --> 00:36:28.480
of several terabytes,
there's a lot of queries

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:29.980
that a scan over
the entire database

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:32.140
is going to take hours, right?

00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:34.820
So we use BigQuery as
our data warehouse.

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:38.620
We really like BigQuery, and
we push both production data,

00:36:38.620 --> 00:36:41.440
so the MySQL data, into
BigQuery, but also event data

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:42.700
we're streaming there.

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:47.630
And so to join these two data
sets, we need to do this copy.

00:36:47.630 --> 00:36:48.866
So how do we do this?

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:54.880
This is a diagram
of this process.

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:58.390
Basically we've
written a go client,

00:36:58.390 --> 00:37:03.550
and all this client does
is it's executed on a cron,

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:06.820
it's scanning through the
entire backup machine,

00:37:06.820 --> 00:37:09.680
and it's streaming
this into a GCS file.

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:14.860
So what we end up with is a
group of files sitting in GCS,

00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:17.616
I believe they're JSON
format, and this is

00:37:17.616 --> 00:37:19.600
an entire copy of our database.

00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:21.741
Once it's in GCS, it
becomes easy, right?

00:37:21.741 --> 00:37:23.740
You press a button, and
you're able to import it

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:24.780
into BigQuery.

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:29.180
And BigQuery actually
handles the table swapping.

00:37:29.180 --> 00:37:32.470
In other words, we have
a new copy of the data,

00:37:32.470 --> 00:37:35.304
we're putting it on
BigQuery, and then

00:37:35.304 --> 00:37:36.970
we need to swap it
in for the old table.

00:37:36.970 --> 00:37:39.302
So BigQuery is
handling that for us.

00:37:39.302 --> 00:37:41.260
So remember, this is a
totally separate system,

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:44.680
and this is executing on a
cron, so this is not up to date,

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:46.031
up to the minute.

00:37:46.031 --> 00:37:47.530
It depends on the
table size, but we

00:37:47.530 --> 00:37:50.410
run this, for some tables,
every day, and for some tables,

00:37:50.410 --> 00:37:51.144
every week.

00:37:51.144 --> 00:37:53.560
So there are separate systems,
and there is a delay there.

00:37:53.560 --> 00:37:55.855
But it's a big win
because you're then

00:37:55.855 --> 00:37:58.464
able to process the
MySQL data in BigQuery,

00:37:58.464 --> 00:38:00.880
and you're able to join that
against our event data, which

00:38:00.880 --> 00:38:02.350
is also sitting in BigQuery.

00:38:02.350 --> 00:38:03.430
So we really like this.

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:04.480
We really like
this architecture.

00:38:04.480 --> 00:38:05.530
We really like
BigQuery, and it's

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:06.696
been really powerful for us.

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:14.544
So as I mentioned, this
is kind of unfinished,

00:38:14.544 --> 00:38:15.960
and there's a lot
of improvements.

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:17.740
We're always iterating on this.

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:20.280
And I just kind of want
to call out something

00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:22.560
that we, internally,
at Quizlet think about

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:26.190
and want to improve.

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:30.480
So one is we want to improve
our scripting around failover.

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:32.670
There's a lot of
complexity here,

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:35.292
but quicker failover is better.

00:38:35.292 --> 00:38:36.750
There are a few
things that we want

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:42.134
to do to increase the speed
with which we can do this.

00:38:42.134 --> 00:38:44.550
And the other thing that we
see is that recovery sometimes

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.580
involves manual steps.

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:52.590
So if replication is off, if
the GTIDs are off, et cetera,

00:38:52.590 --> 00:38:54.462
we've seen cases like that.

00:38:54.462 --> 00:38:55.920
That sometimes
involves a developer

00:38:55.920 --> 00:38:57.540
actually going and
looking at those,

00:38:57.540 --> 00:39:01.730
and those we'd like to
narrow down on and eliminate.

00:39:01.730 --> 00:39:04.040
Another thing that
we see is the larger

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:06.880
GCE instances, live
migration is not

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:10.670
totally transparent for systems
that are running really,

00:39:10.670 --> 00:39:12.730
really heavy transactions.

00:39:12.730 --> 00:39:14.580
And the effect of
this is effectively

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:19.390
your machine just kind
of drops for a second.

00:39:19.390 --> 00:39:25.240
So you'll see-- there will
basically be a live migration

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:28.000
blackout, the machine will
disappear for a few seconds,

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:29.260
then it'll come back.

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:31.390
So this is pretty tricky.

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:36.580
You want to be able
to trust the VM.

00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:39.220
We mitigate this by,
we watch for these.

00:39:39.220 --> 00:39:43.890
So there's a notifier you can
watch that will tell you when

00:39:43.890 --> 00:39:45.160
a live migration is coming.

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:50.020
But fundamentally, you need to
avoid single points of failure,

00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:55.410
and you want to avoid relying
too much on a single machine.

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:58.200
As I said, the bigger machines
are more susceptible to this.

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:00.730
So the smaller machines
you can run, the better,

00:40:00.730 --> 00:40:03.532
in a lot of cases.

00:40:03.532 --> 00:40:04.990
A big problem with
our architecture

00:40:04.990 --> 00:40:08.030
is we haven't
horizontally scaled yet.

00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:10.220
So we're not doing
sharding of a single table

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:14.610
across multiple databases.

00:40:14.610 --> 00:40:16.752
And we've done this
basically to be pragmatic.

00:40:16.752 --> 00:40:18.210
There's a huge
amount of complexity

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:21.840
that comes with managing
sharding yourself.

00:40:21.840 --> 00:40:25.550
There's a lot of solutions
in this space, as well.

00:40:25.550 --> 00:40:28.260
And effectively we've been
able to punt this problem

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:32.100
and shard vertically,
rather than horizontally,

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:34.500
but it represents a pretty
fundamental limitation

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:36.990
on this architecture.

00:40:36.990 --> 00:40:38.580
But one of the things
we're looking at

00:40:38.580 --> 00:40:42.257
is what other technology
we can leverage.

00:40:42.257 --> 00:40:44.340
As you saw, we have the
really strong growth rate.

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:47.820
And so our architecture is
going to continue to change,

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:50.430
and we're going to have
to adapt to the requests

00:40:50.430 --> 00:40:51.780
from users coming in.

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:54.570
So we've been experimenting with
Cloud Spanner as a replacement

00:40:54.570 --> 00:40:58.710
for MySQL in certain
places and an option

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:00.770
for continuing to scale this up.

00:41:00.770 --> 00:41:02.670
And so actually, we
did a talk yesterday

00:41:02.670 --> 00:41:05.700
about what we'd learned about
Cloud Spanner as a replacement

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:08.220
for some of this architecture,
and we have a blog post

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:09.220
about this, as well.

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:12.829
So it's also targeted at
if you're running a MySQL

00:41:12.829 --> 00:41:14.370
architecture, and
you're figuring out

00:41:14.370 --> 00:41:20.560
what the next step of that looks
like, it's intended for you.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:21.980
So that's what I have.

00:41:21.980 --> 00:41:26.650
I hope this was
useful and helpful,

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:29.020
and I think we're going
to take questions.

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:31.082
But if you don't get your
question [INAUDIBLE],

00:41:31.082 --> 00:41:33.040
I'll be around, as well,
and happy to chat more

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:34.240
about this stuff.

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:37.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

